ical record. By unifying IPFS content addressing with multi-dimensional contextual vectors (Spatial, Rule, and Relation), Ed25519 cryptographic signatures, and hash-linked sequence chains, CVP establishes a gasless, lightweight provenance framework natively on IPFS primitives. This protocol converts passive data storage into "Det Levende Arkivet" (The Living Archive)—an autonomous repository capable of continuous data ingestion, real-time cryptographic validation, and verifiable historical record-keeping. Project Metadata Summary The table below synthesizes the core attributes, system configurations, and cryptographic parameters defining the Commons Vector Protocol architecture: Attribute Protocol Specification Value Project Title Commons Vector Protocol (CVP) & Det Levende Arkivet Principal Investigator / Author Kim Terje Rudschinat Grønli Trust Namespace commons-vector-v1 Primary Infrastructure IPFS (InterPlanetary File System) & Pinata Gateway Gateway Infrastructure URL https://yellow-managerial-leopon-627.mypinata.cloud/ipfs/ Native Packaging Format .car / .car.json (Content Addressable Record) Primary Lexical Dictionary TRUST-ORDBOK-v2.0 (bafkreiah7zzhane2iwpijyx7qiuwajqbmaep3a4pnvuo5f4jcakm4ibnzi) Key Cryptographic Primitives SHA-512 (Composite Key derivation), SHA-256 (IPFS Payload CID), Ed25519 (Digital Signatures) Canonical Serialization JCS (JSON Canonicalization Scheme, RFC 8785) / CBOR (RFC 8949) Registry Standardization Master Asset Ledger unifying sequence 001 through 098/099 Core Problem & Technical Solution The Fragility of Legacy Records For centuries, physical registries, paper deeds, state archives, and centralized database records have served as authoritative proofs of ownership, identity, and historical priority. These centralized solutions exhibit critical structural vulnerabilities: * Absence of Provenance Memory: Legacy paper documents and centralized database entries do not inherently record their origin, creation chain, or modification lineage. * Vulnerability to Tampering and Forgery: Centralized registries represent single points of failure. Records can be altered, overwritten, backdated, or deleted by system administrators or malicious actors without leaving an immutable audit trail. * Duplicate Issuance and Conflicting Claims: Centralized databases permit duplicate claims or conflicting titles to be issued for the same asset or identity, resulting in disputes where the claim appearing most official prevails over factual priority. While standard decentralized storage solves single-point-of-failure storage vulnerabilities, a bare IPFS CID identifies payload content but fails to encode where it resides in a namespace, how it is governed, or who signed it in what sequential order. The CVP Solution The Commons Vector Protocol resolves these limitations by binding every piece of content into a multi-dimensional, self-describing vector record. CVP establishes an immutable record system anchored by the mathematical principle that "the first record wins" (de jure within the protocol trust). +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ | CVP RECORD STRUCTURE | | +------------------+ +-------------------+ +---------------+ +--------------+ | | | Content Vector | | Spatial Vector | | Rule Vector | | Relation | | | | (IPFS CID) | | (Namespace Path) | | (Dictionary) | | Vector | | | +--------+---------+ +---------+---------+ +-------+-------+ +------+-------+ | | | | | | | | +----------------------+---------+----------+-----------------+ | | | | | v | | [ Canonical Composite Key ] | | | | | v | | [ Ed25519 Signature Envelope ] | | | | | v | | [ Hash-Linked Provenance Chain ] | +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ Rather than relying on central authorities or external consensus layers, CVP records demonstrate priority and authenticity through direct mathematical proof. A CVP record combines payload content, spatial placement, governance dictionaries, and object linkages into a deterministic composite key. Signed cryptographically and linked sequentially to its immediate predecessor (prev: "GENESIS" through seq N), a CVP record cannot be backdated, reordered, or forged. This architectural foundation underpins the multi-layer ingestion engine that automates data capture from physical environments directly into IPFS storage. 2. Autonomous Ingestion Architecture: "Det Levende Arkivet" System Operational Context To transition IPFS from a manual file-upload destination into a real-time, autonomous record-keeping infrastructure, the Commons Vector Protocol integrates "Det Levende Arkivet" (The Living Archive). Manual file uploads introduce operational friction, human delay, and recording bias. Det Levende Arkivet operates as an automated multi-layer pipeline that captures physical real-world inputs, normalizes them using artificial intelligence, structures them against standardized trust dictionaries, and pins them cryptographically to the IPFS network. The 3-Layer Ingestion Engine [ PHYSICAL LAYER: "Sansene" ] └─► Sensors, Microphones, Cameras, Automated Cryptographic Timestamps │ ▼ (Raw Physical Sensor Inputs) [ AI PROCESSING LAYER: "Hjernen" ] └─► Array of 17 AI Models ├─► Noise Removal & Signal Formatting ├─► Consistency Check against Genesis Trust Dictionary └─► Formatted JSON / Canonical Text Payload Output │ ▼ (Canonical Payload Output) [ CVP PROTOCOL LAYER: "Nerven" ] └─► Tri-Vector Assembly & Cryptographic Engine ├─► Payload Content Upload to IPFS (CID Derivation) ├─► Composite Key Derivation: SHA512(CID : Spatial : Rule) ├─► Ed25519 Signature Wrapping & Provenance Chaining └─► Native .car Archive Pinning via Pinata Gateway 1. The Senses (Sansene / Physical Layer) The physical layer consists of automated real-world collection instruments—including hardware sensors, environmental detectors, microphones, video cameras, or system-generated cryptographic timestamps. These instruments continuously capture raw data directly from physical environments without human intervention, ensuring that recorded events reflect unmanipulated observations. 2. The Brain (Hjernen / AI Processing Layer) An integrated processing engine composed of an array of 17 specialized AI models evaluates the raw sensory streams. The AI processing layer performs four primary functions: * Noise Reduction: Filters environmental noise and signal artifacts from the raw data. * Format Standardization: Translates heterogeneous input streams into normalized data formats. * Semantic Consistency Validation: Validates data fields against the Genesis Trust Dictionary (TRUST-ORDBOK-v2.0 / ILPJ-v1.0.4) to ensure compliant terminology and scope definitions. * Payload Generation: Formats the verified data into canonical JSON or structured text blocks ready for IPFS deployment. 3. The Nerve (Nerven / CVP Protocol Layer) The protocol layer receives the structured output from the AI engine and executes native cryptographic packaging: * Content Addressing: Uploads the normalized payload to IPFS, generating its unique payload CID. * Vector Binding: Combines the payload CID with its assigned spatial namespace address, governing rule dictionary identifier, and relational linkages. * Cryptographic Signing: Derives the canonical composite key and wraps it within an Ed25519 signature envelope. * Chain Appending: Attaches the sequence's prev hash, appends the record to the unbroken provenance chain, and pins the resulting native .car (Content Addressable Record) object across the IPFS network via the Pinata gateway infrastructure. This ingestion architecture converts real-world events into deterministic mathematical vectors. The next section defines the mathematical specification governing these record vectors. 3. Cryptographic Technical Core & Tri-Vector Specification Strategic Importance of Multi-Dimensional Vectoring Standard IPFS addressing uses Content Identifiers (CIDs) that intentionally omit metadata regarding application context, geographical coordinates, governance schemas, or relational lineages. Decoupling content identity from context, governance rules, and location is essential for structured data interoperability. The CVP multi-dimensional vector model enforces this separation at the protocol level. By isolating content identity from spatial placement, lexical rules, and relations, CVP enables IPFS nodes to index, query, and verify records across diverse namespaces without modifying underlying raw data payloads. The Tri-Vector & Relation Specification Every CVP record is constructed around the File-ID3 contextual vector model, which binds four independent dimensions: Vector Dimension Field Identifier Protocol Function & Description Canonical Example Vector 1: Content Vector cid Cryptographic IPFS hash defining what the raw content payload is. Unique, immutable, and content-addressed. bafkreidkpydmulrfnfc5feqlmhpr4ihsscxge7ndt32yduofgdnfau4lge Vector 2: Spatial Vector v1_spatial Namespace address defining where the record resides within the trust hierarchy or coordinate space. trust:commons-vector-v1:living:witness Vector 3: Rule Vector v2_rule Governance schema and lexical dictionary identifier defining how the record is governed and interpreted. ILPJ-v2.0 / format:native; extension:.car; public:true Vector 4: Relation Vector v3_relation Linkage block defining explicit relationships to other objects (derived_from, supersedes, contributes_to). type: derived_from; target: bafkreigq3j6... Composite Key Derivation Formula & Cryptographic Reconciliation The Composite Key serves as the definitive instrument number and unique address of every record in the protocol. It deterministically combines content identity with contextual vectors. In prototype index implementations, the Composite Key was calculated using a string concatenation hash function: \text{Composite Key}_{\text{legacy}} = \text{SHA512}(\text{CID} + \text{":"} + \text{spatial} + \text{":"} + \t