ture pattern in Web3 involves recording IPFS CIDs inside EVM smart contracts (such as ERC-721 or ERC-1155 metadata pointers) to track provenance. CVP presents an architecture that executes provenance natively on IPFS via .car headers and cryptographic vector envelopes, offering distinct performance advantages: +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ | EVM SMART CONTRACT PROVENANCE | | [ IPFS Storage ] ──► CID ──► [ EVM L1/L2 Contract State ] | | • High Gas Costs per Transaction • High Latency (Block Confirmations) | | • Scalability & Throughput Bottlenecks • Metadata Pointer Disconnected On-Chain | +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ VS +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ | CVP NATIVE IPFS PROVENANCE | | [ IPFS Payload ] ──► [.car Envelope + Tri-Vector + Ed25519 + Hash Chain] | | • Zero Gas Costs (Gasless Execution) • Sub-Second Network Propagation | | • Unlimited Horizontal Bandwidth • Self-Contained Native Provenance | +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ * Gasless Execution & Zero Financial Barriers: EVM state updates incur variable transaction fees (gas) for every provenance event. CVP operates entirely gaslessly through cryptographic hash-chaining, enabling high-frequency event logging for resource-constrained environments. * Bandwidth & Latency Optimization: EVM block confirmation times introduce throughput bottlenecks and latency. CVP records propagate at native IPFS network speed, allowing real-time data verification without consensus wait states. * Native Metadata Integrity: EVM smart contracts store CIDs as passive pointers while metadata remains off-chain. CVP encapsulates payload, spatial coordinates, governance rules, signatures, and provenance links directly within a unified, portable .car container. Protocol-Level Boundary & Legal Distinction (§15 Non-Goals) T