Procedural Execution Steps

1. Retrieve Payload: Fetch the target raw payload file from IPFS using its CID via any public node or Pinata gateway (https://yellow-managerial-leopon-627.mypinata.cloud/ipfs/[CID]).
2. Hash Payload: Calculate the local cryptographic hash of the downloaded payload file:
 * Linux/macOS: sha256sum filename
 * Windows PowerShell: Get-FileHash filename -Algorithm SHA256
3. Compare Hash: Convert and decode the CID string into its raw multihash byte array using standard utilities such as cid-tool (cid-tool format -b base32 [CID]) or ipfs cid base32. Compare the locally computed SHA-256 hash against the multihash embedded within the decoded CID.
4. Verify Signature: Execute an Ed25519 signature check verifying the signature.value field against the signature.public_key calculated over the record's canonical number.composite byte string using standard cryptographic tooling (openssl or minisign).
5. Check Provenance Link: Verify that the provenance.prev string strictly equals the number.composite key of sequence record N-1.
6. Re-Compute Composite Key: Re-derive the composite key locally by executing the canonical serialization and hashing derivation: \text{Derived Key} = \text{SHA512}(\text{CID} + \text{":"} + \text{spatial} + \text{":"} + \text{rule}) Confirm that the calculated multihash string matches the number.composite field in the record.
7. Confirm Unbroken Chain: Iterate steps 1 through 6 recursively backward along the provenance chain until reaching sequence 000 / seq: 0 (prev: "GENESIS").

If all seven steps evaluate successfully, the record and its historical lineage are cryptographically valid. The next section evaluates CVP's strategic utility within the broader Web3 ecosystem.

